{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>Any devotee that is devoted from among men.</b> One who is going forth to be executed, and he said, 'Assess my value upon me' — he has said nothing. <b>Shall surely be put to death.</b> He is going to die; therefore he shall not be redeemed, he has no monetary value and no valuation — this is the language of Rashi. And our Sages disagreed concerning this matter (Arakhin 6a), some of them saying that it is a warning concerning those liable to execution, that no ransom is taken from them to free them. And it is possible that this is mentioned here to say that he shall not give money to HaShem like the other devoted things and thereby go free. And according to the plain sense, Scripture says that anyone who devotes from his own property — whether person, animal, or field of his ancestral holding — it is holy to HaShem, for these are devotions to the priests and have no redemption, but one who devotes from a person that is not his own, such as those who wage war against their enemies and vow a vow, 'If you will indeed give this people into my hand, then I will devote their cities' (Numbers 21:2) — every person found among them shall die. And the reason is that the intention of the one vowing is not to give them to the priests, but rather that all of it be forbidden for benefit, for his intention is to annihilate the enemies and destroy them. And we find the men of Jabesh-gilead who transgressed the oath of the assembly and did not come to the muster at Mizpah, and it is written, 'And they sent there the congregation twelve thousand men of valor, and commanded them, saying, Go and strike the inhabitants of Jabesh-gilead with the edge of the sword' (Judges 21:10). And reason does not permit that the entire congregation would do such an evil thing, to put to death many people of Israel who are not liable to death, and Phinehas was there, and by his word all that matter was done. Furthermore, I found in an Aggadah in Yelammedenu: It was taught, Rabbi Akiva says, the herem is the oath and the oath is the herem; the men of Jabesh transgressed the herem and became liable to death. Therefore I say that from this verse this ruling emerged for them: any king in Israel, or the Great Sanhedrin in the assembly of all Israel, who have authority in legal matters — if they impose a herem upon a city to wage war against it, and likewise if they impose a herem regarding any matter — the one who transgresses it is liable to death. And this is the liability of the men of Jabesh-gilead and of Jonathan, for his father said to him, 'Thus shall Elohim do and more also, for you shall surely die, Jonathan' (I Samuel 14:44). And from where were these liable to death by law, if not from this place? And this was the error of Jephthah concerning his daughter, for he thought: just as the herem of a ruler of Israel takes effect and is binding to put people to death, or one who transgresses his herem is liable to death, so too if he vowed in time of war to make a sacrifice of a person or persons, the vow should take effect. And he did not know that the herem of the king and the Sanhedrin takes effect upon rebels to destroy them, or upon one who transgresses their decree and ordinance, but for a vow to take effect to make an offering of something unfit for HaShem — far be it. Therefore it was said in Bereishit Rabbah (60:3), that he was not liable even for consecration of money, and he was punished through her blood. And do not be persuaded by the vanities of Rabbi Eliezer who says that the meaning of 'I will offer it up as an offering' (Judges 11:31) is 'or I will offer it up' — to say: if the one coming out of the doors of my house is a man or woman, it shall be holy to HaShem, that he be set apart from the ways of the world to stand and minister in the name of HaShem in prayer and thanksgiving to Elohim; and if it is a thing fit to be offered, I will offer it up as an offering. And he built a house for his daughter outside the city, and she secluded herself there, and he provided for her all her days, and no man knew her, and his daughter was confined there. And these are words of emptiness, for if he vowed that it would be 'to HaShem,' it is not that he would be set apart, but rather he would be like Samuel, whose mother said, 'and I will give him to HaShem' (I Samuel 1:11), and he ministered in the house of HaShem — not set apart. And according to the laws of the Torah, it is not in a person's power to vow concerning those who go out from the door of his house that they be set apart, just as it is not in his power to offer them up as an offering. And if the matter is so, his daughter bewailing her virginity upon her and her companions with her would be like prostitutes praising their hire, and Heaven forbid that it should be a statute in Israel to go about for the daughter of Jephthah four days a year because she was not married to a husband and served HaShem in purity. But the matter is as it is written simply, and his error was from what I have said. And according to the opinion of our Sages of blessed memory, it is possible that this verse encompasses many matters, like 'You shall not eat upon the blood' (Leviticus 19:26), and like 'Fathers shall not be put to death for children' (Deuteronomy 24:16), which they expound regarding disqualification of the testimony of relatives (Sanhedrin 27b), while Scripture also says elsewhere, 'But the children of the murderers he did not put to death, as it is written in the book of the Torah of Moses, as HaShem commanded, saying, Fathers shall not be put to death for children, and children shall not be put to death for fathers' (II Kings 14:6). So too this verse may encompass the law of one who assesses those liable to death, and the law of one upon whom a herem is imposed at the time of conquest as we have said, and one who transgresses the herem of the Great Court or of the king of Israel, as in the matter of Saul's deed. And this is what is said there, 'But the people redeemed Jonathan, and he did not die' (I Samuel 14:45), for in contrast to what the Torah said concerning this, 'He shall not be redeemed; he shall surely be put to death,' it says there, 'But the people redeemed Jonathan, and he did not die' — for because a miracle occurred through him, they knew that he acted unwittingly. And this is the meaning of 'who has wrought this great deliverance in Israel' (ibid.). And thus Jonathan ben Uzziel rendered it: 'Because before HaShem it was revealed that this servant acted unwittingly on that day, and the people rescued Jonathan.'" "And the Book of the Torah of the Priests and their offerings and their commandments is completed. May El Most High restore things to their former state — Levites to their song and their music, and priests to their service, and Israel to their stronghold dwelling. May our eyes behold Jerusalem, a garden-orchard, and the palace, in its rightful place, the sanctuary and the inner shrine on their foundations; and the daughters of Judah in their tranquility; and may the offering of Judah be pleasing to HaShem as in days of old and years of yore. May there ascend upon the will of His altar the continual offerings in their order and the additional offerings according to their law." "The book of Leviticus is finished and completed, with the help of the Blessed Name."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {0 "The quote from Jonathan ben Uzziel's Targum at the end of the comment follows the Aramaic text as Ramban cites it; the citation of Bereishit Rabbah uses the abbreviation ב\"ר."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:02:00.096386848Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}